00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
thanks to curiositystream and nebula for  sponsoring this video  there's something rather special about  the lab that i'm standing in right now  there's some amazing science experiments  behind me but that's not what makes this  place unique what makes this place  unique  is  what's above us it's an interesting  place and it's very difficult to  describe so you go down this cage and it  takes about seven or eight minutes and  then you're a kilometer underground when  people hear the word mine they think of  i don't know mine's in cornwall small  mines you crawl into baubie is nothing  like that it's huge roadways a thousand  kilometers of roadways cut into this  sort that are so big you can drive a  truck down them it's very salty it's  very dark it's very dry and all those  things give this place a very  otherworldly feel about it you know you  crunch along these tunnels to the  underground lab so you walk through this  very dirty dusty source of that so this  very high tech laboratory it's looking  for dark matter where we do some  astrobiology research and that's the  environment you're in it's an almost  alien environment almost like you would  expect being in a laboratory  on mars would be like disconnected from  the rest of the world  i am 1.1 kilometers under yorkshire in  the north of england at the bolby  underground lab  we exist 1.1 kilometers underground in  this mine here at bulby so the lab here  has existed since the 1990s and  originally a professor from sheffield  university  wanted an underground space to really  start to understand dark matter they  approached the mine and asked basically  can we have some space underground to do  a little bit of science and the mine  were like yeah that'll be fine they you  know they didn't particularly understand  what was going on but it wasn't very  invasive the lab here is is built to  host science from collaborations all  over the world one of the things we can  boast in is that we have a very broad  science program so most underground labs  in the world exist for particle physics  but we we have really established  ourselves as a multi-disciplinary lab we  have amazing environmental science that  goes on we've got the  space technology we've got the  astrobiology work that goes on and  really interesting geological science as  well although some of the other  underground labs are now exploring those  avenues we really paved the way for that  here at bulby  my name is ed banks my job title here is  facility science support technician  which i think has at least one too many  words essentially what that means is  that i am the on-site lead for various  experiments the actual science is owned  by various institutions universities  businesses and they send the science to  us and we run it for them remotely to be  honest i absolutely love it it's not far  from probably a dream job for me getting  to work with  different types of science every day is  amazing so the first thing that you need  to do is get kitted out in mine gear  because it is an active mine and we're  going to be walking through that so you  have your full orange overalls hard hat  safety boots all that sort of stuff um  you then go to the mine building where  you pass through a series of air locks  and this is because there's a huge  overpressure needed to ventilate the  massive tunnel system uh you then get  into the lift which is not an ordinary  lift that you might find in a building  it's a three-story cage  where  some of the walls kind of has have gaps  you can see through to the the tunnel  outside the the cage as we call it then  takes seven minutes to descend the 1.1  kilometers to the level of the mine uh  once you are there you then have to walk  through the tunnels to the lab and  that's just over half a kilometer takes  about 10 minutes  yeah it does feel otherworldly  definitely like walking through the  tunnels and the temperature the dust in  the air like just the texture of the  ground is weird like it's not something  that you used to  feels very strange sometimes a lifelong  ambition of mine has been to be  an astronaut and i've imagined myself  walking on the moon countless times  but  i think this may be the closest i ever  get to actually walking on the moon's  surface it really does feel like we're  exploring the ruins of some  old alien civilization on a distant  planet  this industry that's just coated in a  thick layer of fine powder once you  reach the entrance of the lab you have  to then get clean because you've walked  through a dirty mine so you have to get  changed into the white overalls that you  can see us wearing now different hard  hats we have dirty ones for the tunnel  and clean ones for inside and then you  can come in and do whatever science it  is that you're here to do  so in the background of some of this  video you may hear what sounds like  dance music and it's dance music at  precisely 60 beats a minute  it's not that  it is  behind me  the liquid nitrogen plant so liquid  nitrogen is used in a bunch of science  experiments to cool components down  normally you just sort of ship it in but  because this is a kilometer below ground  that's a little inconvenient so  they make their own here and it just so  happens the machine operates  as emma said a whole range of  interesting sciences done down here but  of particular interest to me was the  research relating to space exploration  and specifically exobiology because for  very specific reasons this mine is a  very good approximation to the red  planet it's shockingly similar to mars  in some ways  i often hear people ask like if you're  doing space research have you not gone  the wrong way because obviously you know  we're underground we believe that on  mars there used to be surface water we  haven't found liquid water yet there's  ice at the poles and but we believe  there used to be surface water features  that have at some distant point in the  past evaporated  so those kind of conditions are  the same as what led to the formation of  the salt in the caverns here so you know  ancient sea evaporated left behind some  salt if there are caverns in that salt  on mars in the just below the surface  and then that would make this even  closer to the sort of conditions that  we'd expect it does feel a little bit  childish sometimes of look at this cool  rock i found but  pretty much a pure salt crystal so what  information about the life that existed  in the ancient sea  can you infer from what's left in the  salt  um  you can like directly look for these  microorganisms oh really almost like an  amber  sort of yeah so one of like the kinds of  experiments that they do is you have  little hammers and you crack open the  rock and then you  put the samples on your microscope and  look for microorganisms  straightforward is that so in some cases  they are like still visible even after  230 million years to find out more i  spoke to professor charles kirkel at the  university of edinburgh who has  performed experiments in astrobiology at  the bulby mine i mean astrobiology some  people think it's looking for aliens  but but astrobiology  is simply looking at biology in its  cosmic context and i know it sounds a  bit strange what i mean by that is it's  also looking at the history of life on  earth we don't have to go and look for  alien life to know there is life on this  planet that's fairly uncontroversial and  so how did it get here and how has it  been around for four and a half billion  years and what is the future of life on  this planet bobby is is  you know a kilometer or more of of salt  and there's no such environment anywhere  actually else elsewhere in the solar  system ironically but um having said  that if you go down into ball b minor  kilometer underground what you will find  is trickles of water seeping through  those ancient salt deposits quarter of a  billion years old and those very salty  deposits are similar to what we do  observe or what we think we observe on  mars and some people think that even in  the present day  there is briny water seeping out from  the subsurface of mars into craters and  so those brines in bourbon might be  similar to the sorts of environments  that existed on mars some of these  brines in baubie if they flow through  the wrong type of salt like magnesium  chloride they develop chemical  conditions that completely prevent the  growth of life so in other words if you  go into one place in bourbon you'll find  brine seeps that are full of life and  then if that brine  goes slightly in the wrong direction  passes through magnesium chloride the  water activity or the availability of  water becomes so low that no life can  grow in that brine and as a consequence  it's completely lifeless it can inform  where you might want to go and look for  life uh it can inform how you might look  for life in those brains let's say that  i gave you a sample of a brine on mars  you know how would you get biological  information out of that brian first  thing i would just stick it under a  microscope so i'm a bit old school like  that you know microbes are easy to see  under a good microscope so if it was a  modern brine that's the first thing i  would do i would stain it maybe with  some  stain that binds to  nucleic acids or proteins and i would  put it under a microscope and see  whether i see little glowing dots then i  would try various chemical extractions  to see whether i could extract dna or  dna like molecules or proteins and then  i might try and look at the lipids the  sorts of things that make up cell  membranes so by looking at the sorts of  life that exists in  martian brines we can get some ideas  about the limits of life on mars and  what might have limited life there but  it's as i say also about trying to  understand the fundamentals you know how  does life adapt to extreme environments  and and what sort of planetary  environments can it live in  and and what sort of environments is it  excluded from and these are interesting  questions purely from a from a  biological point of view as well as the  practical point of view of looking for  life on mars i find it fascinating that  in order to explore other planets we  find alien worlds on our own planet and  after thousands or tens of thousands of  years of habitation we're still finding  these environments that are maybe just a  few kilometers away from what we think  of as our home planet  and that are a lot easier to return from  than mars i don't know if you can hear  this but there's we're walking into  quite a headwind at the moment and  that's because  the mine has to have its air pumped in  right so there's a system  where the air flows in  circulates around and then flows out so  the outflow is really hot and i was just  told  that if you're lost if your light dies  or whatever  good tip  walk into the wind  because it means  you're near  the exit shaft  bulby hosts many fascinating research  projects martian exobiology being just  one of them another key area of study  one kilometer below the earth's surface  is dark matter but what is this stuff  and how are physicists at bolby trying  to find it  well i've made a whole second video  about that over on nebula nebula which  recently passed half a million paying  users is the streaming service owned and  operated by a collection of educational  youtubers here you can watch our videos  without ads on the website or in the  videos as well as additional original  content such as my videos on dark matter  and how norway became the electric  vehicle capital of the world but there's  also original series such as the battle  of britain by real engineering or modern  conflicts by real life law to get access  to nebula you can sign up with our  partners curiosity stream the largest  source of high quality documentaries on  the web if you enjoyed this video then  perhaps you'll enjoy the mini series on  curiosity strea